    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d2">

                <pb facs="0131" n="3" xml:id="pg3"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.5112 France/96: Circular telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Houghton</hi>)<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d2-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-01-06T18:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 6,
                                1928—6 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>An English translation of the proposal which Briand submitted to me last June
                    reads as follows:</p>
                <p>[Here follows the text of the draft pact of perpetual friendship contained in
                    telegram No. 260, June 22, 1927, from the Chargé in France, printed in <ref
                        target="frus:frus1927v02/pg_615"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, 1927, volume II, page 615</ref>.]</p>
                <p>Please deliver to the Foreign Office for the consideration of the Government to
                    which you are accredited a memorandum containing the foregoing text and the text
                    of my reply<anchor n="5" xml:id="d2-f2"/> which was telegraphed to you in the
                    Department’s circular, December 28, 8 p.m.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d2-f1" n="4" type="source" xml:id="d2-f1-content">
                <p>The same telegram was sent to the Embassies in Germany, Italy, and Japan.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d2-f2" n="5" xml:id="d2-f2-content">
                <p>See <ref target="frus1927v02#pg_626"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, 1927, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, p.
                        626</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
